CAT = "txpprDiversity"; SEG = "DIV"
# Competency 002 (student diversity).
Q = [
("A Spanish-speaking kindergartner already knows letter sounds in Spanish. How should the teacher view this knowledge?",
 "A resource for learning English", ["A barrier to learning English well", "Unrelated to reading skills", "A habit to discourage at school"],
 "Many literacy skills transfer from the first language to the second, so building on them speeds English reading."),
("Under the ELPS, an emergent bilingual sixth grader is at the beginning proficiency level in speaking. Which accommodation fits best in a science discussion?",
 "Accept gestures and short phrases", ["Require full-sentence answers", "Grade his spoken grammar", "Excuse him from speaking"],
 "The ELPS proficiency level descriptors say beginning speakers mainly use single words and short phrases. Accepting those, with visuals and modeled language, keeps him in the discussion while his English grows."),
("In Texas, who is responsible for building the English Language Proficiency Standards (ELPS) into instruction?",
 "All teachers of English learners", ["Only ESL-certified teachers", "Only the campus counselor", "Only English language arts teachers"],
 "The ELPS (19 TAC Chapter 74) are to be integrated into every subject by every teacher who has English learners."),
("A student's IEP calls for extended time on assignments. What must the teacher do?",
 "Provide it as written", ["Provide it when convenient", "Ask the class to vote", "Decide if it is needed"],
 "An IEP is a legally binding plan, so teachers carry out its accommodations. Changes go through the ARD committee."),
("In Texas, which group develops a student's IEP under IDEA?",
 "The ARD committee", ["The LPAC", "The site-based committee", "The school board"],
 "Texas calls the IEP team the admission, review and dismissal (ARD) committee. The LPAC serves English learners."),
("A fourth grader identified as gifted also has ADHD and rarely finishes long tasks. Which approach best meets her needs?",
 "Challenge strengths, support needs", ["Treat the ADHD before any enrichment", "Remove her from the gifted program", "Ignore the ADHD since she is gifted"],
 "A twice-exceptional student needs both: advanced work in her areas of strength and supports, such as chunked tasks and check-ins, for the disability. Neither need waits for the other."),
("A teacher plans a lesson with captions, audio and print versions of the text from the start. Which framework is this?",
 "Universal Design for Learning", ["Response to intervention", "Differentiated grouping", "Positive behavior supports"],
 "UDL builds multiple means of representation, engagement and expression into the plan from the beginning instead of retrofitting."),
("A teacher learns that a student's family values group success over standing out. Which approach fits best?",
 "Cooperative learning tasks", ["Ranking students publicly", "Praise in front of all peers", "Grading only individual work"],
 "Culturally responsive teaching draws on students' values; cooperative structures can raise this student's engagement."),
("A student has a disability that limits a major life activity but does not need special education. Which plan can still give accommodations?",
 "A Section 504 plan", ["An individual health plan", "A behavior contract", "A graduation plan"],
 "Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act covers students whose disability substantially limits a major life activity, even if they need no specially designed instruction."),
("A teacher groups students for reading using this week's data and regroups them next week. This practice is:",
 "Flexible grouping", ["Tracking", "Ability labeling", "Fixed reading groups"],
 "Flexible groups change as data change, unlike fixed tracking that keeps students in one group all year."),
("A newcomer from another country sits silently for weeks and only listens. How should the teacher interpret this?",
 "A normal silent period", ["A learning disability", "Defiance of directions", "A hearing problem"],
 "Many new English learners go through a silent period while they build listening comprehension; by itself it is not a sign of disability."),
("A colleague says students from one neighborhood \"can't handle\" advanced work. Which belief should guide the teacher instead?",
 "High expectations with support", ["Easier work for that group", "Advanced work for a chosen few", "Grades based on background"],
 "Equitable teaching holds every student to high expectations and gives the support needed to reach them. Lowering demands for a group of students widens gaps."),
("Students may show what they learned about a novel through an essay, a podcast or a comic, all scored with the same rubric. What is differentiated?",
 "The product", ["The content", "The standard", "The rubric"],
 "Differentiating the product lets students choose how to show learning while the content, the standard and the criteria stay the same for everyone."),
("A student with dyslexia struggles to read the grade-level social studies text. Which support keeps him on grade-level content?",
 "An audio version of the text", ["A lower-grade textbook", "Removal from the lesson", "Extra spelling homework each night"],
 "Audio access lets him learn grade-level content while his reading skills are addressed through intervention."),
("Which Texas committee decides an English learner's program placement and when the student exits?",
 "The LPAC", ["The ARD committee", "The PTA board", "The grade-level team"],
 "The Language Proficiency Assessment Committee reviews proficiency data to place, monitor and exit English learners."),
("A student from a low-income family cannot afford supplies for a class project. What should the teacher do?",
 "Provide materials quietly", ["Lower the project grade", "Assign her a new partner", "Ask her to tell the class"],
 "Equitable access means removing the barrier discreetly, without singling the student out."),
("An English learner chats easily in English but struggles with textbook language. What does this most likely show?",
 "Academic English takes longer", ["She is not trying hard", "She needs special education", "She should stop using Spanish"],
 "Social language often develops within a couple of years, while academic language can take several years more."),
("A teacher pre-teaches key vocabulary to a small group before a whole-class lesson. Who benefits most?",
 "Students with less background", ["Only the advanced readers", "Only students with IEPs", "Students who finish work early"],
 "Frontloading vocabulary gives students with less background knowledge access to the main lesson."),
("A student who uses a wheelchair cannot reach the science lab sink. What must the teacher ensure?",
 "Equal access to the activity", ["A written task instead", "Observation from his desk", "A partner who does it for him"],
 "Students with disabilities have the right to take part in the same activities, so the teacher adjusts the setup rather than excluding him."),
("A teacher notices she calls on boys more often than girls in math. What is the best next step?",
 "A system to call on all", ["Call on volunteers only", "Stop asking questions", "Let boys answer first"],
 "Tracking or randomizing who is called on gives every student an equitable chance to respond."),
]
